You type a command to sort a list for the neighborhood swap.
At home the result looks the way you expect; the same command
on a library computer puts capitals and lower-case letters in
an order you did not anticipate. Neither line of input changed.
One possible difference is the locale inherited by \texttt{sort}.
An ordinary command receives more than its visible arguments:
it can also receive a set of named settings.

\begin{objectives}
\item Predict which shell variables an external command can see.
\item Change a locale setting for a single command without changing the session.
\item Inspect command lookup without replacing or publishing your environment.
\end{objectives}

\section{Pass a setting down, not back up}
\label{sec:28-export}
\index{environment}
\index{export@\texttt{export}}
\index{env@\texttt{env}}

In a fresh \texttt{sh} session, try the following using the made-up
setting \texttt{SWAP\_LABEL}. The first line clears any previous
value and export attribute for that name in the practice shell.
The \texttt{-c} option of \texttt{sh} runs the following argument
as shell source in a new shell. Here that argument is a fixed
line you type yourself. Its single quotes keep the parent
from substituting the value before the new shell starts.

\begin{terminal}
unset SWAP_LABEL
SWAP_LABEL='Kitchen items'
printf 'Parent: <%s>\n' "$SWAP_LABEL"
sh -c 'printf "Child: <%s>\n" "${SWAP_LABEL-unset}"'
export SWAP_LABEL
sh -c 'printf "Child: <%s>\n" "${SWAP_LABEL-unset}"'
\end{terminal}

The parent prints \texttt{<Kitchen items>}. The first child
prints \texttt{<unset>}; the second child prints
\texttt{<Kitchen items>}. This is what the \emph{environment}
is for: it carries named strings to a newly started program.
A shell can use its own variables without putting them in the
environment. \texttt{export} marks an ordinary shell variable
for inclusion in the environment of subsequent external
commands. It does not broadcast that label to programs that
have already started.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-28/inherit.tikz}
\caption{Only the exported setting crosses the process boundary in this example.}
\label{fig:28-inherit}
\VisualDescription{The parent holds a working note and an exported label. Only the exported label has an arrow to the child shell.}
\end{figure}

The receiving program decides what to do with a setting.
A made-up name like \texttt{SWAP\_LABEL} is not a system
instruction: it matters to the second shell only because
we explicitly print it. Programs that agree on conventions
such as \texttt{PATH} or \texttt{LC\_ALL} give those strings
particular effects. Looking up the setting in the manual
is better than guessing from its name.

\begin{terminal}
sh -c 'SWAP_LABEL=changed; printf "<%s>\n" "$SWAP_LABEL"'
printf 'Parent: <%s>\n' "$SWAP_LABEL"
\end{terminal}

The child prints \texttt{<changed>}; the parent still prints
\texttt{<Kitchen items>}. The semicolon in the child source
separates two commands executed one after the other. The
child has its own environment rather than a live reference to
the parent's variables. A program can write a file that the
parent later reads, but assigning a variable is not such a
communication channel.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Why didn't it stick?}
\Brian{I set the label in the child, and it printed the new label. Why did the parent ignore the change?}
\Stephen{The child got a copy. It can change that copy without anyone else's permission.}
\Brian{But the shell has a builtin called export. Wouldn't exporting from the child send it back?}
\Stephen{That makes the child's future commands inherit its new setting. It still doesn't send the setting to the parent.}
\Brian{So to keep it for the next command I type, change it at this prompt?}
\Stephen{Yes. Or give just that next command a temporary value if the others shouldn't get it.}
\DialogueConclusion{Settings pass to newly started children.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Borrow a value for one command}
\label{sec:28-prefix}
\index{locale!per-command setting}
\index{sort@\texttt{sort}}

Write an assignment directly before the name of an external
command to give that command a value without changing the
calling shell's variable. This chapter uses external commands
for the technique: shell functions and some special builtins
have additional rules. Run both these lines with the earlier
label still exported:

\begin{terminal}
SWAP_LABEL='Outdoor items' sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "$SWAP_LABEL"'
sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "$SWAP_LABEL"'
\end{terminal}

The first shows \texttt{<Outdoor items>}; the second still
shows \texttt{<Kitchen items>}. Unlike the earlier assignment
by itself, the prefix does not store the new label in the
calling shell. The external command here is \texttt{sh}.
Do not replace the fixed text after \texttt{-c} with a filename
or a string someone else sent you; this option treats its
argument as a shell command, not data to be printed.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-28/override.tikz}
\caption{A prefix supplies an override for just one external command.}
\label{fig:28-override}
\VisualDescription{The parent stores Kitchen items. One call has an Outdoor items override and sees it. The next call has no override and sees Kitchen items.}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:28-override} is useful for locale settings.
The portable \texttt{C} locale is suitable for this ASCII
comparison, where we deliberately want the uppercase
\texttt{Apple} before lowercase \texttt{apple}:

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' apple Apple Book | LC_ALL=C sort
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{Apple}, \texttt{Book}, \texttt{apple},
one per line. The pipe connects the left command's standard
output to \texttt{sort}; on the right side \texttt{LC\_ALL=C}
sets the locale for \texttt{sort}. It does not change the
locale of the left command or of your session. Without the
override, the same order is possible, so observing that
order by itself does not prove which locale was used.
For names intended to be read in a person's language, the
preferred locale may be their usual one rather than
\texttt{C}. Reproducible order and familiar reading order
are different goals.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.3in}YY}
\toprule
Form & Who sees the value & Typical use \\
\midrule
\texttt{name=value} alone & The current shell; future commands too if that variable was already exported & Store a value for subsequent shell expansion. \\
\texttt{export name} & Subsequent external commands inherit the value, if set. & Set a session-wide agreement with tools you will run. \\
\texttt{name=value command} & The external command and programs to which it passes the setting. & Override a tool's locale for one invocation. \\
\texttt{unset name} & Removes the current value and its export attribute. & Clear a practice variable without editing startup files. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

There is a small trap when a command line both changes a setting
and reads it back. In \cmd|SWAP_LABEL=new printf '%s\n' "$SWAP_LABEL"|
the expansion in the argument is performed by the \emph{calling}
shell, before the command receives the new setting. That makes
it a bad test of inheritance even on a shell that supplies the
prefix value to \texttt{printf}. Use the child-shell example
instead, so the recipient actually looks up the value itself.

\section{Look up the command before adjusting the search}
\label{sec:28-path}
\index{PATH}
\index{command@\texttt{command}}

\texttt{PATH} is the list of directories used when searching
for an external command written without a slash. A colon
separates directory names. The usual search tries the
directories in order; different installations may have
different directories and different versions of a utility.
Builtins and shell functions can be found without that directory
search; aliases can also change how a line is read. Begin with
observation rather than editing \texttt{PATH}:

\begin{terminal}
command -v sort
command -v cd
command -v sh
\end{terminal}

\texttt{command -v} reports how the shell identifies a
command. Typically the first and last lines are executable
paths, whereas the middle line is \texttt{cd}, naming a
builtin. The exact paths are local evidence, not values to
copy from another computer's instructions. A command name
typed with a slash, such as \texttt{./some-tool}, names
a path rather than requesting a search through \texttt{PATH}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-28/search.tikz}
\caption{Directory order matters once an external command search begins.}
\label{fig:28-search}
\VisualDescription{A first directory lacks the command, so the search moves to the second. The second has the command; the third is not searched in this example.}
\end{figure}

If a different executable of the same name appears as you
move from one computer to another, record what
\texttt{command -v} reports on each computer and compare their
manuals. Do not start by changing startup files to fix a
single command. And avoid putting \texttt{.} or an empty
component into \texttt{PATH}: the current working directory
should not unexpectedly supply a program just because you
navigated into a folder. This chapter makes no permanent
changes to command lookup.

\begin{quicktable}{YY}
\toprule
Clue & What you can safely check \\
\midrule
Tool is not found & Is it installed? Does \texttt{command -v} see it in this shell? \\
Command runs but an option is rejected & Does the path point to the implementation whose manual you read? \\
Order changes between computers & Does the sample give the same result with the same per-command locale? \\
Works at the prompt but not in a child & Did the parent export the setting? Does the child use it? \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Show only what someone needs to see}
\label{sec:28-privacy}
\index{privacy!environment}

A plain \texttt{env} command lists environment entries.
Do not include that dump in a bug report, in a public
screenshot, or in a transcript you paste into a chat.
An environment can hold private paths, account names and
credentials. It is enough here to print an invented entry
using an exact variable name. Passing secrets in command
text is not a substitute: the command may appear in history,
process diagnostics or logs. Consult the application's
documented credential mechanism for a real tool.

\begin{terminal}
env SWAP_LABEL=Preview sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "$SWAP_LABEL"'
\end{terminal}

With a variable assignment and utility operands, \texttt{env}
invokes the utility with that setting in its environment.
Here the output is just \texttt{<Preview>}. The example is
an alternative to shell prefix syntax, not a way to isolate
a command from all other inherited settings. The POSIX
\texttt{env -i} form starts the selected command with a
cleared environment before adding any supplied settings;
do not use that form on a real application without checking
what configuration the application needs.

\section{Reproduce a difference without showing your whole setup}
\label{sec:28-reproduce}

If you need to tell a colleague what the sort command did,
send a tiny input you invented and the exact setting that
matters. Run the following experiment in the fresh practice
shell rather than showing the output of \texttt{env}.
Use the C locale to produce a baseline on both systems.
This toy list has no private names or attached information.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' chair Chair Book | LC_ALL=C sort
printf '%s\n' chair Chair Book | sort
\end{terminal}

The first run should display \texttt{Book}, \texttt{Chair},
\texttt{chair}; the second depends on the session. If both
runs agree, you have not ruled out a locale difference for
all inputs. You have learned that the chosen input does not
distinguish the environments. If the baseline agrees but
the unqualified runs differ, check the locale's
collation setting, which governs ordering. Other locale
categories govern other parts of interpretation. A single
known setting, supported by a sample that reveals the
difference, is better diagnostic evidence than a large dump.

You can also see whether a value survives deliberate
environment clearing without running a configurable
application. In the following recipe both commands use an
absolute interpreter path so their lookup does not rely on a
cleared \texttt{PATH}. Run this only if \texttt{/bin/sh}
exists; the example assumes its conventional location.

\begin{terminal}
env -i /bin/sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "${SWAP_LABEL-unset}"'
env -i SWAP_LABEL=Travel /bin/sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "${SWAP_LABEL-unset}"'
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{<unset>} followed by \texttt{<Travel>},
regardless of the parent value. This demonstrates named
environment settings, not full isolation: the child can
still start in the same working directory and run under
your account. Some applications derive defaults even when
the corresponding entries are missing. So do not treat
\texttt{env -i} as a security boundary or expect a command
to behave exactly as if it were on a different computer.

When sharing your diagnostic, distinguish the lines you
ran, the output you observed, and the result you expected.
Report \texttt{LC\_ALL=C} as a choice in the first command,
not as a new default for your computer. A lookup path
under your home directory could disclose your account
name; use an unmistakable placeholder in the report
rather than deleting the fact that it came from your home.
Retain the difference between a system executable and a
user-installed executable if that difference is part of the
problem. The point of redaction is to preserve the useful
evidence while withholding the details unrelated to the
question.

\section{Choose where a setting should live}
\label{sec:28-scope}

When deciding how widely to set a value, imagine the
next command you will run. If only one comparison needs
the C locale, write it directly on that comparison.
If several consecutive shell commands really do need
the same setting, use a new practice shell, export the
setting there, and exit when finished. Changing a startup
file is a different decision: it could affect every
future session that reads that file, including a session
you open for an unrelated task. You do not need the
largest scope to solve the smallest problem.

Try it with a harmless recording convention. This
pair of reads prints the parent value before and after
running a group in parentheses. The shell runs that
group in a subshell environment, so the change doesn't
survive into the calling shell. The parentheses are
shell syntax in this example, not punctuation in the
label.

\begin{terminal}
SWAP_LABEL='Kitchen items'
printf 'Before: <%s>\n' "$SWAP_LABEL"
(
    export SWAP_LABEL='Children'
    sh -c 'printf "Inside: <%s>\n" "$SWAP_LABEL"'
)
printf 'After: <%s>\n' "$SWAP_LABEL"
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{Kitchen items}, \texttt{Children}, and
\texttt{Kitchen items}. \texttt{export name=value} both
assigns and marks the variable in one action. This time
that action is inside the group; the inner \texttt{sh}
gets the value that is current there. The finish of the
group does not change the stored setting outside it.
Like a single-command override, this is useful for a
short-lived choice; unlike a prefix assignment, the
group can include more than one command. Neither is
needed simply to print the label in your current prompt:
ordinary shell expansion already handles that.

There is another distinction worth recording. If you
want someone to select the file to print, an explicit
argument makes the choice visible in the invocation.
If the application needs to follow a general locale
convention, it makes sense to inherit the setting.
Where both are available, use the precedence documented
by the application. The shell passes the arguments and
environment along; it does not decide on behalf of the
application which of them should win. This is why a
successful child-shell test only establishes delivery
to the environment, not that a completely different
tool reads or interprets the setting as you expect.

\section{Compare a shell setting with a command lookup}
\label{sec:28-lookup}

Suppose a teammate reports finding a different copy of a
utility. You can use the same test case to distinguish
two questions: ``What did this shell resolve?'' and
``What would another shell resolve with the same
environment?'' Try the following with \texttt{sort};
it reads no input and starts no copy of \texttt{sort}.

\begin{terminal}
command -v sort
sh -c 'command -v sort'
\end{terminal}

On a typical installation both identify the same
external utility. They need not be identical if your
current shell has a function or alias that changes
the first lookup, or the new shell's startup
configuration changes its environment. A match
establishes that the same spelling was resolved in
this test, not that every command in a more involved
recipe has the same identity. Repeat the comparison
on the first utility whose behavior actually differs.

When reading \texttt{PATH}, it can be tempting to start
with its entire printed value and search it by eye.
But not every directory in the list has the utility,
and ordinary command lookup can also remember a path
from a former search. If you recently installed
another version, a fresh shell is a useful independent
check, provided you know which configuration it reads.
Compare the paths it reports before considering a
persistent change. If both report the intended path
and the options still differ, the manuals or version
details are the next place to look; adding directories
will not turn one implementation's options into another's.

For a documented repeatable task on one computer, an
explicit executable path can settle which installed
copy to use. It can also tie the recipe to that one
computer. An executable in a user's private folder
will not magically appear on a collaborator's system.
If the recipe needs to travel, document which utility
and features you require, let the reader locate their
copy, and use examples small enough to compare. This
is more useful than pasting a long \texttt{PATH} from
one account into someone else's startup file.

\section{Exercises and cleanup}

\begin{exercise}{Distinguish a local variable from an export}
\label{ex:28-export}
Store \texttt{Swap this Saturday} under the invented name
\texttt{SWAP\_NOTICE}. Ask a new shell to print it before and
after you export it. Work in the fresh practice shell.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
unset SWAP_NOTICE
SWAP_NOTICE='Swap this Saturday'
sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "${SWAP_NOTICE-unset}"'
export SWAP_NOTICE
sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "${SWAP_NOTICE-unset}"'
unset SWAP_NOTICE
\end{terminal}
Expect \texttt{<unset>} then \texttt{<Swap this Saturday>}.
The last line removes the practice value.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Compare two matching invocations}
\label{ex:28-sort}
Sort the same three ASCII words twice. Put the locale directly
before the command the first time and supply it using
\texttt{env} the second time. Keep the fixture entirely in
the pipeline; do not create a file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' book BOOK chair | LC_ALL=C sort
printf '%s\n' book BOOK chair | env LC_ALL=C sort
\end{terminal}
Both runs print \texttt{BOOK}, \texttt{book}, then
\texttt{chair}. There is no file or persistent setting to
clean up.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Check what an override leaves behind}
\label{ex:28-override}
With the chapter's label still exported, give one command the
value \texttt{Glassware}. Then verify that the calling shell
still has the value \texttt{Kitchen items}.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
SWAP_LABEL=Glassware sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "$SWAP_LABEL"'
printf '<%s>\n' "$SWAP_LABEL"
\end{terminal}
The two bracketed values differ. The override was limited to
the first invocation, so there is no extra setting to remove.
\end{solution}

Remove \texttt{SWAP\_LABEL} using \texttt{unset SWAP\_LABEL}
or exit the practice shell. Nothing was written to disk.

\begin{takeaways}
\item A child gets an environment from its parent but cannot change the parent's variables by assigning its own.
\item Set a locale for one external command when you need a controlled comparison.
\item Observe search results and report only the settings relevant to the problem.
\end{takeaways}

